WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.077
The paper studies a few key knobs.

00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:07.597
The most important is the optional
topological-ordering restriction: enforcing an input

00:00:07.677 --> 00:00:12.926
ordering enlarges the variable domain slightly but
shrinks the overall search space,

00:00:13.006 --> 00:00:17.177
reducing solve time compared to the fully
unrestricted formulation.

00:00:17.257 --> 00:00:22.443
The per-node interval budget, called C, is fixed at
two throughout the experiments,

00:00:22.523 --> 00:00:25.235
which the authors note in every plot legend.

00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:30.945
The complexity table makes the contrast concrete:
Checkmate's Boolean variable count grows

00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:34.688
quadratically in the number of nodes plus a
node-edge term,

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:39.954
while Moccasin's integer variable count grows only
linearly in the number of nodes,

00:00:40.034 --> 00:00:41.540
with a constant factor C.
